      sources: "Sources: Chapter 02, “The earliest end”; Book 03, Appendix A, Contractor leavers.",
      say: `The heart of the chapter is the earliest end. For each register row, the extract computes every end condition that applies and takes the first: the contract end or an earlier completion date, suitability withdrawn, a card terminated, revoked or expired, a sponsor gone past the grace period, or a sponsor recertification that's due. The thirty-day grace period and the hundred-and-eighty-day cycle are design guidance the agency sets. A record past its end goes out inactive, and every record carries its end as the termination date, so the leaver fires that day.`,
      detail: `extract computes, for each register row, every end condition that applies and takes the first. The conditions and their order are Book 03's (Appendix A, Contractor leavers); the sponsor's 30-day grace period and the 180-day recertification cycle in config/contractor-rules.json are design guidance the agency sets. A record whose end is on or before the run date goes out INACTIVE, so the provisioning service disables the account; every record carries its end as the termination date, so the leaver workflow (Chapter 03) fires on that day. This chapter adds python/contractor_source.py, config/contractor-rules.json and the contractor-reconcile task. examples/hcm-entra already builds and sends the SCIM bulk requests; its mapping already treats worker type C as a contractor with code CTR. The validation kit reports enabled member users with no employeeId (VK-HR-01) and the worker type fill rate (VK-HR-07).`,
