      say: `The reconciliation makes one Graph call: a filtered read of contractor users with their leave dates, needing two read permissions. Then the sponsor review. The review command writes the review settings for the contractor package's assignment policy: every six months, the requestor's manager as reviewer, a named fallback, and remove access on no response. So a missed recertification ends access, just as the earliest-end rule says. The code sends nothing. Learn's cmdlet takes the whole policy, so you merge the block into the policy you read back.`,
      detail: `The reconciliation's one Graph call: GET /users filtered to employeeType eq 'Contractor', with employeeLeaveDateTime; least privilege User.Read.All and User-LifeCycleInfo.Read.All, carried from the validation kit's VK-HR-05 notes; the filter needs ConsistencyLevel: eventual and $count. review writes assignment-policy-review.json: the reviewSettings block for the contractor package's assignment policy, bi-annual (absoluteMonthly, interval 6), the requestor's manager as primary reviewer, a named fallback reviewer and removeAccess on no response, so a missed recertification ends access as the earliest-end rule says. Learn documents the requestorManager subject set for approval stages; its use as a reviewer in reviewSettings isn't shown on Learn, and Lab 2 confirms it (verification log, row 10). The code sends nothing. Learn's cmdlet for updating a policy in v1.0 is Set-MgEntitlementManagementAssignmentPolicy, which takes the whole policy as its body, with EntitlementManagement.ReadWrite.All; merge the block into the policy you read back and send that. The output's own _target note names the same cmdlet.`,
