      sources: "Sources: Chapter 02, “Dangers.”",
      say: `The dangers all come down to access outliving the reason for it. A trusted spreadsheet end date lets access outlive the engagement; the computed earliest end stops it. An open-ended contractor never leaves, so it's quarantined. A sponsor who left leaves nobody accountable, so access ends after the grace period. A missed recertification removes access. A drifted leave date is caught, and so is a card for someone nobody sponsors. And a second SCIM client means two writers, so everything goes through the one middleware.`,
      detail: `A trusted spreadsheet end date: access outlives the engagement; guard: the earliest end from every condition; CR-01. An open-ended contractor: no leaver ever fires; guard: CS-01 quarantines the record. A sponsor who left: nobody is accountable for the contractor; guard: CS-03, and the end after the grace period. A missed recertification: access stays because no one answered; guard: removeAccess on no response, and the recertification date as an end condition. A leave date in the directory that drifted: the leaver workflow fires on the wrong day; guard: CR-05. A card for someone the source doesn't hold: a credential works for a person nobody sponsors; guard: CR-03, and Chapter 07's contractor credential drift. A second SCIM client: two writers, two sets of validation; guard: the extract goes through examples/hcm-entra; the register names one writer (Chapter 01).`,
